The `tokenizerId` property. In that test (in `packages/core/test/cache/…` — the §17 provider-cache-simulator suite), the construction and assertions are:

```ts
const bytes = new ProviderCacheSimulator({
  tokenizer: new ExactTokenizer('bytes-v0', (text) => text.length),
  ...
});
...
expect(bytes.tokenizerId).toBe('bytes-v0');
expect(bytes.total).not.toBe(heuristic.total);
expect(bytes.segments.map((s) => s.blockIds)).toEqual(heuristic.segments.map((s) => s.blockIds));
```

So the assertion is `bytes.tokenizerId === 'bytes-v0'` — it pins that the simulator reports the exact tokenizer's ID it was constructed with, while the two tokenizers' *totals* differ (`bytes.total !== heuristic.total`, since exact byte counts diverge from the heuristic estimate) even though their *segment structure* (the serialized `blockIds` sequence) must match.

